# Docker / Kubernetes / Terraform patterns

Structural patterns for containerized workloads, complementing `rules/coding-style.mdc`.

## Multi-stage build as the default shape

The stage that has a compiler, dev dependencies or test tooling never ships; the
`runtime` stage receives only what `COPY --from=build` carries over.

## Config and code stay separate

A hardcoded hostname, replica count or connection string in a Deployment template
or `.tf` resource block belongs in a `values.yaml`, `ConfigMap` or `variable` block.

## Least-privilege as the default

- A Dockerfile ends with a non-root `USER`, or keeps the base image's non-root
  default un-overridden; see `rules/security.mdc`.
- A Kubernetes workload requests only the RBAC verbs/resources it calls; flag a
  ServiceAccount bound to a namespace-wide `edit`/`cluster-admin` role.
- A Terraform IAM/role resource is scoped to the specific actions and resource
  ARNs needed, not a wildcard `"*"` action or resource.

## Idempotent, declarative infrastructure

- A Terraform module is safely re-appliable: no `null_resource` or `local-exec`
  provisioner for a resource the provider already models, and no resource
  depending on something outside Terraform state (a manually created bucket
  referenced by name instead of imported or created by the module).
- A Kubernetes manifest describes the desired end state; prefer `kubectl apply -f`
  (or a GitOps controller) over hand-run `create`/`patch` sequences in a CI script.

## Module and environment layout

One Terraform module per reusable resource group (`modules/vpc`,
`modules/eks-cluster`) instantiated per environment (`environments/staging`,
`environments/production`), not one flat set of `.tf` files gated by
`count`/`var.environment == "production"` conditionals.
